# --- Rust / Cargo ---
# Not /target/ - packages outside the workspace (tools/pad-tables) have their own target
# directory, and a root-anchored rule silently misses them.
target/
# **And a lane's own target directory, whatever it called it.** Several instances build at
# once and each keeps its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` so they do not evict each other's artifacts -
# this lane's is `target/claude`, which the rule above already covers, and `tools/spec/target2`
# appeared beside it. **An untracked build directory in `git status` is not merely noise**: the
# hazard this repository guards hardest is staging something you did not mean to, and the
# defence is reading `git status` before every `git add`. A list with a build tree in it is one
# nobody reads.
#
# Nothing tracked begins with `target`, measured before writing this, so the glob takes only
# build output.
target*/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# --- Trunk (the WASM bundle) ---
# Built by CI and published from there; a local copy is never the one that ships.
dist/

# Cargo.lock is committed for binaries/workspaces with binaries.
# If this ever becomes a library-only repo, uncomment the next line.
# Cargo.lock

# --- JetBrains (RustRover / IntelliJ) ---
.idea/
*.iml
*.iws
*.ipr

# --- Visual Studio Code ---
.vscode/
!.vscode/extensions.json

# --- Operating system ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# --- Editors / misc ---
*.swp
*~

# --- Sean's own working notes ---
# Renamed from notes/ on 2026-09-01 and untracked at the same time: these are transient,
# and a transient file in the history is one a later reader has to work out the status of.
temporary-notes/
